  body: "# Partner income: attribution and household exception\n\n**Model-assisted source interpretation;
    unreviewed. No entitlement decision or executable rule.**\n\nThe captured paragraph says to calculate
    a partner's income in the same way as the claimant's and attribute it to the claimant, while its note
    identifies a household exception involving immigration control.\n\n## Evidence and scope\n\n[DMG 85016,
    PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/690b7db588a98da87e292365/dmg-ch-85.pdf#page=7)
    supports this scoped proposal. The source PDF SHA-256 is `fe6fe79a4ff52271d6edf97af6b01ebd4290867b30d6288c38eb5e3a6d613c74`.\n\
    \nHousehold membership, immigration status, scope and cited legal provisions need separate review.
    No personal information is collected or assessed.\n\nThe capture date, the publication page's update
    date and a date stated in the guidance have different meanings. Linked legislation and memos need
    their own provision, version and applicability review.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/pc-partner-meaning
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source relates partner status to income attribution and expressly retains an
      exception, so the association is not an unconditional rule for every person called a partner.
    evidence:
    - page: page/85/0007
      quote: "85016 In a claim for SPC, calculate income of the claimant’s partner in the same way as
        for the claimant 1.\n\nThe income should then be treated as the claimant’s income 2.\n\n\nNote:
        In SPC a member of a couple is referred to as a partner and both members are referred to as\n\n\
        partners3. DMG 77117.6 gives guidance that a person subject to immigration control should be treated\n\
        as not being a member of the same household. The income of a partner subject to immigration control\n\
        should not be treated as the claimant's income.\n\n\n                                        \
        \       1 SPC Regs, reg 14; 2 SPC Act 02, s 5; 3 SPC Regs, reg 1(3)"
      locator: DMG 85016
